ariables\n- **Group-wise Mean Target Value Analysis** for categorical variables","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# eda_df = X_train.copy()\n# eda_df['TargetValue'] = y\n\n# corr_cols = [\n#     'TargetValue',\n#     'OperationalHoursMeter',\n#     'ManufactureYear',\n#     'TransactionYear',\n#     'TransactionMonth',\n#     'AssetAge',\n#     'DescriptorLength',\n#     'HasOperationalHours',\n#     'HasVariantModifier',\n#     'Intensity'\n# ]\n\n\n# corr = eda_df[corr_cols].corr()\n\n# plt.figure(figsize=(8,6))\n# sns.heatmap(corr, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', fmt='.2f')\n# plt.title('Correlation Matrix')\n# plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.223118Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.22332Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.66685Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.223301Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.666227Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Numerical Summary Analysis\n\n-  `HasVariantModifier` shows the highest positive correlation meaning machines with variants fetch high prices whereas `AssetAge` corresponds to highest negative correlation meaning older vehicles usually cost less.\n- `ManufactureYear` also exhibits a strong positive correlation with `TargetValue`, suggesting that newer machines generally command higher selling prices\n- `OperationalHoursMeter`, `TransactionYear`, and `TransactionMonth` show very weak correlations with the target variable, indicating limited linear relationships with selling price.\n- Overall, the numerical variables display relatively weak correlations with the target, suggesting that categorical features may play a larger role in determining machine prices.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# features = [\n#     'InventoryGroupDescription',\n#     'FunctionalClassification',\n#     'RegionCode',\n#     'UtilizationTier'\n# ]\n\n# fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(18, 12))\n# axes = axes.flatten()\n\n# for ax, feature in zip(axes, features):\n#     top_categories = (\n#         eda_df.groupby(feature)['TargetValue']\n#         .mean()\n#         .sort_values(ascending=False)\n#         .head(10)\n#     )\n\n#     top_categories.plot(kind='bar', ax=ax)\n#     ax.set_title(f'Average Target Value by {feature}', fontweight='bold')\n#     ax.set_ylabel('Average Target Value')\n#     ax.set_xlabel('')\n#     ax.tick_params(axis='x', rotation=45)\n\n# plt.suptitle('Impact of Categorical Features on Target Value',\n#              fontsize=16, fontweight='bold')\n\n# plt.tight_layout()\n# plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.667658Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.667914Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.227269Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:55.667893Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.22657Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Categorical Summary Analysis\n\nThe average selling price varies noticeably across different inventory groups and functional classifications, indicating that machine type and operational category have a substantial influence on equipment value. **Track Type Tractors, Motor Graders, and Wheel Loaders** tend to command the highest average prices.\n\nRegional differences in average selling price are present but comparatively smaller, suggesting that geographic location has a weaker impact on price than machine characteristics.\n\nA clear relationship is observed between utilization level and selling price. Assets categorized under **High Utilization** exhibit the highest average prices, followed by **Medium** and **Low Utilization** tiers. This suggests that utilization-related factors may play an important role in determining equipment value.\n\nOverall, the categorical features appear to provide stronger discriminatory power for predicting selling price than the numerical variables examined in the correlation analysis.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Model Building","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Cross-Val Split\nCreating a train test split of 80-20%\n\nWe are going to work with a `log-transformed` scale for our target variable because as we observed earlier, we have a rightly skewed variable and we dont want our models to be dominated by the outliers and get biased.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"X = X_train.copy()                \ny_raw = train_df['TargetValue']\ny = np.log1p(y_raw)\n\nX_train, X_val, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=4524)\n\ny_train_raw = y_raw.loc[X_train.index]\n\nprint(X_train.shape)\nprint(X_val.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.228093Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.228355Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.4792Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.228331Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.47856Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Encoding Categorical Variables","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The categorical features are encoded using **Ordinal Encoding**, which assigns a unique integer to each category.\n\n`Important Insisight:` Target Encoding on select few variables was explored but no permutation of target encoding yielded a better result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"cat_cols = X_train.select_dtypes(include=['object']).columns\nnum_cols = X_train.select_dtypes(include=['int','float64']).columns","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.479953Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.480183Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.512297Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.480162Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.511367Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\nordinal_cols = [c for c in cat_cols]\n\n\nordinal_pipeline = Pipeline([\n    (\"encoder\", OrdinalEncoder(handle_unknown=\"use_encoded_value\", unknown_value=-1))\n])\n\npreprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n    transformers=[\n        (\"ordinal\", ordinal_pipeline, ordinal_cols)\n    ],\n    remainder=\"passthrough\",\n    verbose_feature_names_out=False\n)\n\ntrain_index = X_train.index\nval_index = X_val.index\noriginal_cols = X_train.columns.tolist()\n\nX_train = preprocessor.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_val = preprocessor.transform(X_val)\n\ntransformed_cols = (ordinal_cols + [c for c in original_cols if c not in ordinal_cols])\n\nX_train = pd.DataFrame(X_train, columns=transformed_cols, index=train_index)\nX_val = pd.DataFrame(X_val, columns=transformed_cols, index=val_index)\n\nX_train = X_train[original_cols]\nX_val = X_val[original_cols]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.513487Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.51374Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.341716Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:56.513711Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.340869Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Scaling","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Tree-based models are scale-invariant and do not require scaling and therefore, we will not perform any scaling.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Model Training","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Creating 5 different models: \n\n- Decision Trees\n- Random Forest\n- CatBoosting regressor\n- XGBRegressor\n- LightGBM","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"models = {\n    'Decision Tree': DecisionTreeRegressor(random_state=4524),\n    'Random Forest': RandomForestRegressor(random_state=4524),\n    'XGBRegressor': XGBRegressor(n_estimators=10000,learning_rate=0.03,max_depth=7,subsample=0.8,colsample_bytree=0.8,tree_method='hist',enable_categorical=True,eval_metric='rmse',random_state=4524, verbosity = 0),\n    'CatBoostRegressor': CatBoostRegressor(random_state=4524,iterations=10000,learning_rate=0.03,depth=6,loss_function='RMSE',eval_metric='RMSE',od_type='Iter',od_wait=150,verbose=False),\n    'LightGBM': LGBMRegressor(n_estimators=10000,learning_rate=0.03,max_depth=7,num_leaves=64,subsample=0.8,colsample_bytree=0.8,random_state=4524,n_jobs=-1,verbose=-1)\n}","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.342683Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.342981Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.351099Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.342946Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.350367Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = []\n\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    X_tr, X_v = X_train, X_val\n\n    model.fit(X_tr, y_train)\n    preds = model.predict(X_v)\n    preds = np.maximum(preds, 0)\n    rmsle = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_val, preds)) \n    results.append([name, rmsle])\n\nresults_df = (\n    pd.DataFrame(results, columns=['Model', 'RMSLE'])\n    .sort_values('RMSLE')\n    .reset_index(drop=True)\n)\n\nresults_df","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.351915Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.352443Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-26T10:41:58.270677Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-26T10:34:57.352417Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-26T10:41:58.269958Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Comparing Model Performance\n\nTo identify the most suitable regression model for predicting vehicle prices, five tree-based machine learning algorithms were trained and evaluated on the same preprocessed dataset. \n\n### Initial Model Configuration\n\n| Model | Key Hyperparameters |\n|--------|---------------------|\n| **Decision Tree Regressor** | `random_state=4524` |\n| **Random Forest Regressor** | `random_state=4524` |\n| **XGBoost Regressor** | `n_estimators=10000`, `learning_rate=0.03`, `max_depth=7`, `subsample=0.8`, `colsample_bytree=0.8`, `tree_method='hist'`, `enable_categorical=True`, `eval_metric='rmse'`, `random_state=4524` |\n| **CatBoost Regressor** | `iterations=10000`, `learning_rate=0.03`, `depth=6`, `loss_function='RMSE'`, `eval_metric='RMSE'`, `od_type='Iter'`, `od_wait=150`, `random_state=4524` |\n| **LightGBM Regressor** | `n_estimators=10000`, `learning_rate=0.03`, `max_depth=7`, `num_leaves=64`, `subsample=0.8`, `colsample_bytree=0.8`, `random_state=4524` |\n\nThe models were evaluated using **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**, where a lower score indicates better predictive performance.\n\n### Model Performance\n\n| Rank | Model | RMSLE |\n|------|----------------------|--------:|\n| 1 | XGBRegressor | **0.202871** |\n| 2 | LightGBM | 0.204812 |\n| 3 | CatBoostRegressor | 0.211271 |\n| 4 | Random Forest | 0.216213 |\n| 5 | Decision Tree | 0.306496 |\n### Observations\n\n- The **XGBoost Regressor** achieved the best performance with an RMSLE. Its combination of a low learning rate (`0.03`), a large number of boosting rounds (`10000`), moderate tree depth (`7`), and row/column subsampling (`0.8`) enabled it to capture complex relationships while minimizing overfitting.\n- **LightGBM** delivered nearly identical performance, demonstrating that gradient boosting frameworks are particularly well-suited for this prediction task. The use of `num_leaves=64` and controlled tree depth allowed the model to learn intricate patterns efficiently.\n- **CatBoost Regressor** also performed competitively with an RMSLE of. Its ordered boosting strategy and built-in overfitting detector (`od_type='Iter'`, `od_wait=150`) contributed to strong generalization performance.\n- **Random Forest** significantly outperformed the single Decision Tree, highlighting the effectiveness of ensemble methods in reducing variance and improving prediction accuracy.\n- The **Decision Tree Regressor** produced the highest RMSLE, indicating that a single tree was insufficient to model the complexity of the dataset and was more prone to overfitting.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nAmong all the evaluated models, the **XGBoost Regressor** achieved the lowest RMSLE and consistently outperformed the other algorithms followed by **LightGBM**. However, since **Random forest** is a different type of model than the boosting models,hence we will also consider this. ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`Important Insight`: This combination was selected after trying all permuations of top 4 models and this combination yielded best result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# HyperParameter Tuning","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Finetuning the top three models to extract best performance:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# def rmsle(y_true, y_pred):\n#     y_pred = np.maximum(y_pred, 0)\n#     return np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred))\n\n# rmsle_scorer = make_scorer(rmsle, greater_is_better=False)\n\n# models = {\n#     \"Random Forest\": (\n#         RandomForestRegressor(random_state=4524),\n#         {\n#             'n_estimators': [300, 500, 800],\n#             'max_depth': [20, 30, None],\n#             'min_samples_split': [2, 3, 5],\n#             'min_samples_leaf': [1, 2],\n#             'max_features': ['sqrt']\n#         }\n#     ),\n\n#     \"XGBRegressor\": (\n#         XGBRegressor(\n#             random_state=4524,\n#             objective='reg:squarederror',\n#             n_jobs=-1\n#         ),\n#         {\n#             'n_estimators': [5000, 8000, 10000],\n#             'max_depth': [6, 7, 8],\n#             'learning_rate': [0.02, 0.03, 0.05],\n#             'subsample': [0.8, 0.9],\n#             'colsample_bytree': [0.8, 0.9],\n#         }\n#     ),\n\n#     \"LightGBM\": (\n#         LGBMRegressor(\n#             ra